ocuments/s57878/january-budget-202627-and-mtfs-202630.pdf","committee":"Healthier Communities and Older People Overview and Scrutiny Panel","date":"27/01/2026","key":true}],"read_documents":[],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-24","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-14","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-19","committee":"Cabinet","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-11","committee":"Children and Young People Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-12","committee":"Governance Standards and Audit Committee","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-17","committee":"Sustainable Communities Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-18","committee":"Council","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-03","committee":"Governance Standards and Audit Committee","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Sustainable Communities Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Pensions Committee","time":"7.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Council","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Children and Young People Overview and Scrutiny Panel","time":"7.15 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Merton faces \u00a392m schools funding gap as government covers 90% of deficit but reform plan approval uncertain","lead":"Merton Council's Cabinet heard on Monday that the cumulative deficit in the dedicated schools grant \u2014 the government funding for schools \u2014 has reached \u00a392.401m, with the schools-funding crisis now substantially offset by a new government grant. The High Needs Stability Grant will cover up to 90% of eligible deficits, leaving Merton to fund approximately \u00a39.2m of the \u00a392.401m shortfall. The grant is contingent on the council's school improvement plan for children with special educational needs being approved by government; Merton submitted its plan on 19 June. Separately, the council's savings programme is falling behind: it has achieved 77% of its \u00a313.177m target so far this year, a sharp decline from 95% last year, with particular shortfalls in children's services and environment budgets. The council is also facing emerging pressures in adult social care placements, children's residential care, and temporary accommodation.","iso":"2026-09-21","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Merton defers borrowing until 2028 as CHAS windfall funds ambitious building plans","lead":"Merton Council's capital programme \u2014 its spending on long-lived assets such as buildings and equipment \u2014 will push its underlying borrowing need up 22 per cent to \u00a3139.5m by 2028, but the council will not take on new debt until 2028 or later. Instead it is funding capital spending from \u00a3195.3m in investments, two-thirds of which came from the 2023 sale of CHAS Ltd, a housing company it owned. The council has held only \u00a338m in external debt since 2007. It is also actively managing its existing LOBO loans \u2014 bank debt where the lender can propose higher interest rates on set dates \u2014 repaying \u00a34m in April at a saving of \u00a3239,000 a year, and planning further de-risking of its remaining \u00a336m LOBO portfolio. The treasury strategy acknowledges that borrowing will be needed once cash balances deplete, but defers that reckoning to the medium term.","iso":"2026-09-16","paper_title":"LBM Treasury Managment Strategy Annual Review 2025-26- FULL COUNCIL","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59618/59618.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","headline":"PFI reserve depletion with three school buildings due for handover in 2029/30, requiring substantial future funding","detail":"The council's PFI reserves are diminishing, and three Local Authority-maintained school buildings are due for handover from the Private Finance Initiative in 2029/30. The council has established a PFI Oversight Board with senior representation from Children's and Finance to monitor risks and implement strategic actions to manage costs. Specialist advice is being sought.","category":"capital","matters":"School building handovers from PFI typically require significant capital investment; this is a known future liability approximately 3.5 years away that could constrain the council's capital programme and may require external funding or borrowing.","figures":"Three Local Authority-maintained schools; handover date 2029/30","quote":"Finally, the diminishing PFI reserves and the associated potential handover of the buildings of three LA-maintained schools in 2029/30 require a strategic approach to obtaining specialised advice and preparing for the handover.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","headline":"Temporary accommodation overspending \u00a31.523m as post-pandemic housing pressures persist","detail":"The Housing and Sustainable Development directorate forecasts an overspend of \u00a31.523m in total, with temporary accommodation alone at \u00a31.149m unfavourable. This reflects the post-pandemic housing crisis and ongoing demand for temporary accommodation, replicated across London and many councils nationally.","category":"demand","matters":"Temporary accommodation is demand-driven and statutory; costs escalate rapidly if homelessness demand increases; this is a sustained structural pressure rather than a one-off cyclical issue.","figures":"\u00a31.523m total Housing directorate overspend; \u00a31.149m temporary accommodation unfavourable variance","quote":"Temporary accommodation is forecasting \u00a31.149m unfavourable, whilst the directorate position is \u00a31.523m unfavourable.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care placements forecasting \u00a34m adverse variance due to rising complexity and more intensive care packages","detail":"Net placements in Adult Social Care forecast a \u00a34m adverse variance as of June 2026, driven by more residents presenting with higher and more complex needs requiring intensive commissioned support in homecare, residential care, nursing care and complex packages. The council notes that detailed placement expenditure data will be analysed at Period 4 (July) and income data at Period 5 (August). A focused recovery plan is in place including reablement, early intervention, reviews of high-cost packages, and stronger approval controls.","category":"demand","matters":"Adult social care is highly demand-sensitive and placement costs accelerate with complexity; this is a widening pressure nationally (\u00a3715m national overspend for 2025/26, with 80% of councils overspending), driven by ageing population and market sustainability issues.","figures":"\u00a34m adverse variance on placements; \u00a3539k total Adult Social Care overspend (Period 3); prior year 2025/26 was \u00a31.009m overspend","quote":"Net placements are forecasting a \u00a34m adverse variance as of June 2026 (P3).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","headline":"Planned savings delivery slipped to 77%, with \u00a33.045m shortfall against \u00a313.177m target, down sharply from 95% in prior year","detail":"Against a \u00a313.177m savings target for 2026/27, the council forecasts achieving only \u00a310.132m (77%), a \u00a33.045m shortfall. Shortfalls are concentrated in Children's services (44% shortfall, \u00a31.580m), Environment (41%, \u00a3500k), and Innovation and Change (39%, \u00a352k). This represents a significant deterioration from 2025/26, when the council achieved 95% of its savings target, leaving only a \u00a3317k shortfall.","category":"savings","matters":"Combined with demand-driven overspends, underdelivery of planned savings means the council will need to draw on reserves or find alternative savings, reducing financial flexibility; the sharp decline from 95% to 77% signals execution risk in transformation programmes.","figures":"2026/27: \u00a313.177m target, \u00a310.132m forecast (77%), \u00a33.045m shortfall; 2025/26: achieved 95% of target (\u00a3317k shortfall only)","quote":"Forecast to be achieved by end of 2026/27  77 %","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-21","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","headline":"Children's Social Care overspend of \u00a33.084m driven by growing demand and increasing complexity in residential placements","detail":"Children's Social Care and Youth Inclusion forecasts a \u00a33.084m overspend at Period 3, driven by sustained growth in demand and increasing complexity of residential placements, particularly in Children with Disabilities services. Corporate Parenting (foster and residential) is \u00a31.269m overspent; Family Support and Safeguarding \u00a31.507m overspent. The council is pursuing mitigation including establishing an in-house children's home and working with local residential and semi-independent providers to improve placement matching and reduce reliance on high-cost external operators.","category":"demand","matters":"Children's placements are demand-driven and outside council's immediate control; increasing complexity suggests costs per placement are escalating, compounding pressure; this trend is national and driven by market conditions and for-profit operators.","figures":"\u00a33.084m total overspend; \u00a31.269m Corporate Parenting; \u00a31.507m Family Support and Safeguarding","quote":"The Children's Social Care budget is forecasting an overspend of \u00a33.084m at Period 3, primarily driven by sustained growth in demand and increasing complexity across the residential placement budget.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-21"},"name":"Merton","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-21","since_iso":"2026-08-22","distress":false,"headline":"PFI reserve depletion with three school buildings due for handover in 2029/30, requiring substantial future funding","lead":"School building handovers from PFI typically require significant capital investment; this is a known future liability approximately 3.5 years away that could constrain the council's capital programme and may require external funding or borrowing.","also":["9 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-09-21","n_findings":10,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Financial Monitoring Report-Quarter 1 2026.27-Cabinet","top_paper_url":"https://merton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s59639/59639.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":51,"news_drafts_total":2}}